---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";

const built = formatDate(meta.builtAt);
const files = [
  {
    href: withBase("/data/statements.csv"),
    name: "statements.csv",
    what: `One row per tracked agency (${meta.counts.agencies} rows): roster, coverage status, currency, the Standard report card, and the current profile, flattened for a spreadsheet or dataframe. Start here.`,
  },
  {
    href: withBase("/data/statements.json"),
    name: "statements.json",
    what: `The same ${meta.counts.statements} statements with structure intact: the full current profile (including commitment texts) plus every revision's classification, model-written summary and field-level profile deltas.`,
  },
  {
    href: withBase("/data/timeline.json"),
    name: "timeline.json",
    what: `Every observed change as a flat event list (${meta.counts.revisions} events, newest first), each with its classification and summary.`,
  },
  {
    href: withBase("/data/adoption.json"),
    name: "adoption.json",
    what: "The monthly adoption series behind the policy page's charts, plus every agency-level concept transition with the revision it happened in.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Data downloads"
  description="Download the tracker's full dataset — every agency, statement profile, revision and classified change — as CSV and JSON, without cloning the repository."
>
  <div class="page data prose stack">
    <h1>Data downloads</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything on this site is generated from one dataset, and you can have it. These
      files are rebuilt with every deploy (this copy: {built}), so they always match the
      pages. No API key, no repository clone, no scraping required.
    </p>

    <h2 id="files">The files</h2>
    <dl class="files">
      {
        files.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              <a class="mono" href={f.href} download>
                {f.name}
              </a>
            </dt>
            <dd>{f.what}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2 id="reading-csv">Reading the CSV</h2>
    <p>
      Columns fall into five groups. <span class="mono">abbr</span> through{" "}
      <span class="mono">originality</span> are the roster: agency name, portfolio,
      whether the policy binds it (<span class="mono">scope</span>), coverage status,
      statement URL, when we first saw it, how often it has changed, and its lexical
      originality score. <span class="mono">stated_first_published</span> through{" "}
      <span class="mono">annual_review_overdue</span> are currency: the statement&rsquo;s
      own dates against the policy&rsquo;s review rules. The{" "}
      <span class="mono">standard_*</span> columns are the report card &mdash; which of the
      Standard&rsquo;s eight minimum elements the statement shows. The remaining columns
      are the current profile in the closed vocabularies of{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/blob/main/src/aps_ai_tracker/profiles.py"
        >profiles.py</a
      >, with list-valued fields (usage patterns, domains, measures, named tools) joined
      by semicolons and <span class="mono">profile_model</span> naming the model that read
      it. Agencies with no published statement keep their roster columns and leave the
      rest empty &mdash; a blank profile means &ldquo;no statement&rdquo;, not
      &ldquo;answered no&rdquo;.
    </p>

    <h2 id="caveats">What to know before you analyse</h2>
    <p>
      The profile fields, change classifications and summaries are a language
      model&rsquo;s reading of each statement &mdash; convenient, mostly right, and worth
      spot-checking against the linked text before you publish a finding. <a
        href={withBase("/reading")}>Reading the data</a
      > explains what each figure claims and, more importantly, what it does not. The
      record also only starts when the tracker did: a statement&rsquo;s first tracked
      revision is not its first published version.
    </p>
    <p>
      The schemas follow the extraction code and do evolve. Each JSON file carries{" "}
      <span class="mono">generatedAt</span>, <span class="mono">headSha</span> and{" "}
      <span class="mono">schemaVersions</span> so you can tell which vintage produced
      your copy; new CSV columns are appended rather than reordered.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-here">What is not here</h2>
    <p>
      The statement texts themselves. Full markdown bodies for every revision live in the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/tree/main/statements"
        >git repository</a
      >, and the current corpus is mirrored to the <a href="https://atproto.com"
        >AT Protocol</a
      > network (the open network Bluesky is built on) under{" "}
      <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/apsaitracker.app">@apsaitracker.app</a>: one
      record per statement and one per tracked change, each with a permanent identifier
      that doesn&rsquo;t depend on this website &mdash; or the agency&rsquo;s &mdash;
      staying online. You can <a
        href="https://pdsls.dev/at://did:plc:yhnshyrc2iev6z65u3uraon4"
        >browse the records</a
      > directly, or subscribe to the network&rsquo;s live event stream to be notified
      of changes the moment they are recorded. The passage-reuse clusters behind the{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/propagation")}>propagation explorer</a> are also derivable from
      those texts.
    </p>

    <h2 id="terms">Reuse and citation</h2>
    <p>
      The dataset &mdash; these downloads and the derived fields in them &mdash; is
      licensed{" "}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, the same
      licence most Australian Government open data carries: use it freely with
      attribution. The scraper and this site are MIT-licensed, and the statement texts
      remain the work of their agencies, most themselves licensed CC BY 4.0. If the data
      feeds something published, cite the tracker via the
      repository&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/blob/main/CITATION.cff"
        >CITATION.cff</a
      >, and check the <a href={withBase("/about")}>about page</a> for the fuller
      licensing story.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .files {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .files div {
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .files dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .files dd {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
